    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="6" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.6b18345</English>
                <French>v15.6b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="18345">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b18345</English>
                <French>v1.16b18345</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Living room ceiling along inside wall area is showing lots of air leakage/thermal bridging. I was unfortuantley not able to get into the attic because of all the snow that was on the roof. My guess is that the vapor barrier was not properly installed or there was some batt insulation that has fall off from that area. If you ever do check this area, make sure to check that the vapor barrier is sealed and taped on the warm side of the insualtion. 



Patio door is showing quite a bit of air leakage around door and around trim as well. May want to take a piece of the trim off and check if there are any places that is not sealed properly. Spray foam is the easiest way to seal around door and window frames.



There are also other places on the flat ceiling that is showing air leakage/ thermal bridging such as:



Master bath ceiling



Back left corner of attic (master bedroom)



Bedroom ceiling.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Add R-15 of Type 2 EPS Insulation to the exterior of the home. This will improve the energy rating of the house for the following reasons:



Reduce amount of gas needed for heating.

Improve comfort in the home.

Decrease heat loss through walls.

Reduce amount of thermal bridging in areas that are hard to insulated (i.e. studs, top plates, bottom plates, corners.)

Will help make the home more air tight.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on insulation upgrades and the criteria for these products.

</mainWalls>
                <VentilationSystem>If there are significant air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as an HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with air qualtiy, mold, and mildew.



The HRV/ERV will recover heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. Make sure the unit is ENERGY STAR® rated and HVI certified.



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>If you are looking to upgrade your hot water heater, you have a couple options.



Option 1:



Install an on-demand instantaneous condensing gas water heater. Make sure this has a minimum Energy Factor (EF) of 0.92 and is ENERGY STAR® rated.



Option 2:



Install a combi hot water loop off the boiler (at 0.79 EF). This could only be installed if a combi boiler system was installed.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on Heating/DHW upgrades and the criteria for these products.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>As the furnace increases in age, its performance will slowly deteriorate. When it comes time to upgrade your heating system, you will have a few different options. 



Install a digital or smart thermostat to help reduce fuel consumption.



Option 1:



Upgrade furnace to an ENERGY STAR® condensing gas furnace with a 95% AFUE. Make sure this furnace has an energy efficient ECM Motor as wel.This system will have direct supply/exhaust which will eliminate the need for the fresh supply air duct in the furnace room. This will decrease the amount of gas burned during the heating season and will reduce cold air entering the home. 



Only cut off the supply for combustion air if both heating and hot water are direct vented/condensing.



Option 2:



Upgrade boiler and hot water tank to a Combi Boiler/DHW system. This would combine your heating and hot water into one system. Make sure to look for one that is ENERGY STAR® and has at least a 95% AFUE rating. It also has direct supply/exhaust.



The boiler will be paired with a fan coil which will allow the heat from the boiler to be distributed by the existing furnace ductwork.</HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
